id=a52sxq_home_depth_clock
name=Galaxy A52s 5G: home-screen depth clock (off by default, switch in UN1CA UI settings)
author=kusti420
description=Samsung's own lock screen clock on the home screen, between the wallpaper and the cut-out person or object of a layered wallpaper (Gallery -> Set as wallpaper -> Home screen). Off by default; Settings > UN1CA > UI settings > Home screen depth clock. Editor (Wallpaper & style, DressRoom 3.0.10.38 patched by dressroom.sh, shipped as the system priv-app with versionCode 1301038000 so the Galaxy Store copy is dropped at boot and no longer offered): the Home screen editor gets a Clock entry in its menu that turns the clock on and off and shows Samsung's lock clock item behind the cut-out subject (tap it for the clock panel: style, font, weight, colour, date and weather; drag, resize, stretch); the home clock saved last (style, place, size, stretch on/off and heights) is where the next Home editor starts. The editor also lets the picture be zoomed out to 50 % and tilted up to 45 degrees (snaps to level), the uncovered area filled with the picture's border colour or a blurred copy of it; that is baked into the saved wallpaper and comes back when the same wallpaper is edited again (Settings.System k4_home_clock_xf, matched to Samsung's saved crop and the picture). Frame layouts (Frame, frame Suggests) keep Samsung's own editor: no zoom-out/tilt/fill there, Samsung's subject view and frame bottom are drawn again above the clock, and the engine puts the clock below the subject (above the frame plate when the subject stays inside the frame). Done stores the clock (Settings.System k4_home_clock_item = AOD SDK ClockItem JSON, k4_home_clock_item_rect, k4_home_clock_bundle) and the switch (persist.sys.k4.home_depth_clock) and tells the wallpaper (com.k4.homeclock.action.RELOAD). Engine (SpriteWallpaper, customize.sh): for the home-only layered wallpaper the engine factory (c3.a.a) gets HomeClockPresenter instead of FlattenLayeredPresenter (h3.d); it flattens the layers into a background and a subject bitmap and draws the clock between them: AODService's clockpack lock screen clock, loaded in-process (UfClockProvider through a package context) and styled and placed as saved, with the date line's weather from the plugin's own weather manager; a plain text clock when nothing is saved or the plugin fails. Clock and subject are drawn after the dark mode wallpaper dim (the subject dimmed to match). It redraws only on the minute tick while the home screen is visible (no wakeups, no per-frame drawing). LayeredEngine onCreate / onVisibilityChanged / onDestroy keep a per-engine state: the RELOAD receiver (senders need WRITE_SECURE_SETTINGS) rebuilds the engine in-process, and the editor's waits for the new wallpaper's engine instead. A home and lock shared layered wallpaper keeps its stock rendering unless persist.sys.k4.home_depth_clock_shared=1. Settings rows (settings.sh, from post_mods.sh): the switch, then Clock position (Automatic / Top / Middle) and Clock colour (White / Theme colour) for the plain clock, replaced by Clock style (opens Wallpaper & style) once the editor saved a clock. The AOD clock permissions LockStar holds are requested by SpriteWallpaper and the signature|privileged ones allowlisted in privapp-permissions-com.samsung.android.wallpaper.live.xml.
